# OMI–IMO / US?-O_o Notation Protocol

## 1. Primitive paired notation

The complete protocol object is:

```text
omi---imo . US?/O_o
```

The dot is the pairing operator:

```text
(A . B)
```

Therefore the complete notation object is:

```text
(omi---imo . US?/O_o)
```

Interpretation:

```text
omi---imo
 defines the scoped state surface

US?/O_o
 defines the temporal gauge traversal

.
 binds the scoped surface to its traversal
```

The object is not a receipt and does not mean accepted state.

It is a **paired computational notation**:

```text
scope configuration
.
temporal transition configuration
```

---

# 2. `omi---imo`: the scope rail

The scope rail contains:

```text
FS
GS
RS
US
```

Their Blackboard meanings are:

```text
FS = Known Known
GS = Known Unknown
RS = Unknown Known
US = Unknown Unknown
```

The four scope states are arranged as two paired halves:

```text
omi-(FS . GS)-(RS . US)-imo
```

This is the expanded form of:

```text
omi---imo
```

Structure:

```text
omi
 │
 ├── (FS . GS)
 │ known-origin side
 │
 ├── (RS . US)
 │ unknown-origin side
 │
imo
```

More exactly:

```text
(FS . GS)
=
known-source pair

(RS . US)
=
unknown-source pair
```

The complete scope rail is:

```text
omi
 open scope boundary

(FS . GS)
 forward epistemic pair

(RS . US)
 reverse epistemic pair

imo
 reflected scope boundary
```

Blackboard form:

```text
 RESULT
 known unknown
 ┌────────────┬────────────┐
SOURCE known │ FS │ GS │
 │ KK │ KU │
 ├────────────┼────────────┤
SOURCE unk. │ RS │ US │
 │ UK │ UU │
 └────────────┴────────────┘
```

So the linear notation:

```text
omi-(FS . GS)-(RS . US)-imo
```

is the folded one-dimensional reading of the two-dimensional Blackboard.

