# 7. XOR of two complete notation objects

The first expression is:

```text
(omi---imo . US?/O_o)
XOR
(omi---imo . US?/O_o)
```

Because any bit-pattern XOR itself is zero:

```text
X XOR X = 0
```

the complete paired object self-cancels:

```text
(omi---imo . US?/O_o)
XOR
(omi---imo . US?/O_o)
=
(NULL . NULL)
```

In protocol notation:

```text
scope XOR same scope
=
NULL

gauge XOR same gauge
=
NULL
```

Therefore:

```text
(A . B) XOR (A . B)
=
(A XOR A . B XOR B)
=
(NULL . NULL)
```

This is the folded null of the complete notation pair.

It does not erase the definition of the notation alphabet.

It means that the two supplied instances contain no difference.

---

# 8. Component-wise XOR law

For two arbitrary paired notation objects:

```text
(A . B) XOR (C . D)
```

the component-wise law is:

```text
(A XOR C . B XOR D)
```

Therefore:

```text
(
 omi---imo₁
 .
 US?/O_o₁
)
XOR
(
 omi---imo₂
 .
 US?/O_o₂
)
```

resolves as:

```text
(
 omi---imo₁ XOR omi---imo₂
 .
 US?/O_o₁ XOR US?/O_o₂
)
```

The left result is the scope difference.

The right result is the temporal-gauge difference.

This distinction is essential:

```text
left XOR
 asks which epistemic positions differ

right XOR
 asks which temporal or orientation positions differ
```

